"スコットランド王 (ファレーズ条約)": "King of Scots (Treaty of Falaise)",
"シチリア・ナポリ王国 (教皇の封)": "Kingdom of Sicily and Naples (papal fief)",
"vassus / fidelis (忠誠を誓う者)、feudum (封)、hommage (臣従礼)、auxilium et consilium (助力と助言)、「封建制 (féodalité)」は 17 世紀の法学者の言葉で当時の言葉ではない (Reynolds)": "vassus / fidelis (one who swears fealty), feudum (fief), hommage (homage), auxilium et consilium (aid and counsel); “feudalism (féodalité)” is a word of 17th-century jurists, not of the period (Reynolds)",
"法 (慣習と誓い) では、封臣は領主に忠誠 (領主の敵と結ばない)・軍役 (40 日の騎士役)・付帯金 (長子の騎士叙任・長女の婚姻・身代金)・宮廷への出席を負い、領主は封を保護し、相続には承認と相続料 (relief)・未成年の後見・没収がある。重罪は領主の裁判 (1174 年のファレーズ条約で英王がスコットランド王の上級の裁判権を得た)。実態は私戦が普通で、付帯金は滞り、強い封臣 (ノルマンディー公) は王と対等に近かった。Fulbert of Chartres の手紙 (1020 年ごろ) が義務を 6 つの言葉で書いている": "De jure (custom and oath), the vassal owed the lord fealty (no alliance with the lord's enemies), military service (40 days of knight service), aids (the knighting of the eldest son, the marriage of the eldest daughter, ransom) and attendance at court; the lord protected the fief, and succession involved approval and a relief, wardship of minors, and forfeiture. Felonies went to the lord's court (by the Treaty of Falaise in 1174 the English king gained superior jurisdiction over the King of Scots). De facto, private war was common, aids fell into arrears, and strong vassals (the Duke of Normandy) were nearly the king's equals. A letter of Fulbert of Chartres (c. 1020) sets out the obligations in six words",
"Fulbert of Chartres のアキテーヌ公ギヨーム 5 世への手紙 (1020 年ごろ。Behrends 編 1976、Letter 51)、『Libri Feudorum』(12 世紀、ロンバルディア)、キエルジーの勅令 (877 年 6 月 14 日)、ファレーズ条約 (1174 年 12 月 8 日)、ル・グレ条約 (1200 年 5 月 22 日)、Recueil des actes des rois de France、Regesta Regum Anglo-Normannorum": "Fulbert of Chartres's letter to Duke William V of Aquitaine (c. 1020; ed. Behrends 1976, Letter 51), “Libri Feudorum” (12th century, Lombardy), Capitulary of Quierzy (June 14, 877), Treaty of Falaise (December 8, 1174), Treaty of Le Goulet (May 22, 1200), Recueil des actes des rois de France, Regesta Regum Anglo-Normannorum",
"Ganshof 1944『Qu'est-ce que la féodalité?』(封と家臣の古典的な定義) と Bloch 1939〜40『封建社会』(社会全体の型) → Brown 1974「The Tyranny of a Construct」(AHR 79-4) と Reynolds 1994『Fiefs and Vassals』(「封」と「家臣」の一般像は史料に合わない。法学者の作り物)。Bisson 1994「封建革命」の論争 (P&P 142)。論点: 「封建制」という型を使ってよいか、使うなら何を指すか": "Ganshof 1944, “Qu'est-ce que la féodalité?” (the classic definition of fief and vassal) and Bloch 1939–40, “Feudal Society” (a type for a whole society) → Brown 1974, “The Tyranny of a Construct” (AHR 79-4) and Reynolds 1994, “Fiefs and Vassals” (the general picture of “fief” and “vassal” does not fit the sources; it is a jurists' construct). The debate over Bisson 1994, “The Feudal Revolution” (P&P 142). Debates: may “feudalism” be used as a type, and if so, what does it denote",
"誓いと人格の結びつき (領主の人が代わると結び直す)。土地の封 (封土) そのもの。ゲームの星系は封土にあたるが、人の忠誠と土地の保有を分けない": "The bond of oath and person (renewed when the lord changes). The landed fief itself. The game's star systems correspond to fiefs, but personal loyalty and landholding are not separated",
