"藩 (当時は「国」「家中」「領分」。「藩」は明治の言葉)、大名・家中、公儀、参勤交代、改易・転封・減封、末期養子": "han (at the time “kuni”, “kachū”, “ryōbun”; “han” is a Meiji-era word), daimyo and kachū (retainer band), kōgi (public authority), sankin-kōtai (alternate attendance), kaieki, tenpō, genpō (attainder, transfer, reduction of domains), deathbed adoption (matsugo yōshi)",
"法 (武家諸法度) と実態が近い例。外交は幕府が独占し (対馬の朝鮮・薩摩の琉球・松前のアイヌは例外)、軍役・参勤交代・普請 (公共事業) を負い、藩内の課税と裁判は藩が持ち、法度に反する法は作れない。家督は幕府の承認が要り、改易・転封で任免と継承を幕府が握った (末期養子の禁は 1651 年に緩む)。寺請・宗門改は幕府の制度を藩が運用。藩札と専売は幕府の統制下。「国内」の統治なので従属国の型と呼ぶかは論争": "A case where de jure (the Buke Shohatto) and de facto are close. The shogunate monopolized foreign relations (exceptions: Tsushima with Korea, Satsuma with Ryukyu, Matsumae with the Ainu); domains owed military service, alternate attendance and public works (fushin); taxation and justice within the domain belonged to the domain, but it could not make laws contrary to the Shohatto. House succession required shogunal approval, and through attainder and transfer the shogunate held appointment and succession (the ban on deathbed adoption eased in 1651). Temple registration and religious inspection were shogunal institutions run by the domains. Domain notes and monopolies were under shogunal control. Whether to call this “domestic” rule a dependency type is debated",
"武家諸法度 (元和令 1615 年、寛永令 1635 年: 参勤交代の制度化)、『徳川実紀』、『寛政重修諸家譜』(大名の家の継承)、各藩の藩政史料 (加賀藩『加賀藩史料』、鹿児島県史料『旧記雑録』)、国立公文書館・国立史料館のデジタルアーカイブ": "Buke Shohatto (Genna code 1615; Kan'ei code 1635: alternate attendance institutionalized), “Tokugawa Jikki”, “Kansei Chōshū Shokafu” (succession of daimyo houses), domain administrative records (Kaga: “Kaga-han Shiryō”; Kagoshima Prefecture records: “Kyūki Zatsuroku”), digital archives of the National Archives of Japan and the National Institute of Japanese Literature (Archives of Japanese History)",
"Hall 1966・Totman 1967 (幕藩体制は「中央集権的封建制」) と、朝尾直弘・藤井譲治の「公儀」論、Ravina 1999『Land and Lordship in Early Modern Japan』(藩は「国」だった)。論点: 幕藩体制は封建制か、藩は国家か。ヨーロッパの型を当てることの是非": "Hall 1966 and Totman 1967 (the bakuhan system as “centralized feudalism”) versus the kōgi theory of Asao Naohiro and Fujii Jōji, and Ravina 1999, “Land and Lordship in Early Modern Japan” (domains were “states”). Debates: was the bakuhan system feudal, were domains states, and is it right to apply European types",
"参勤交代 (人質と出費の制度) の中身。ゲームでは「軍事 M・課税 V・任免 M」の束で代える": "The substance of alternate attendance (a system of hostages and expense). The game substitutes a bundle of “Military M, Taxation V, Appointment M”",
